---
import Flow from "@/components/builders/Flow.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { builderPages, recordedOf } from "@/lib/builders";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { repoFile } from "@/lib/site";

const facts = Object.fromEntries(builderPages.map((p) => [p.slug, recordedOf(p.slug)]));
const stat: Record<string, string> = {
  "esm-cdn": `${facts["esm-cdn"].facts.plotModules} modules for Observable Plot, the first one in ${facts["esm-cdn"].facts.coldMs} ms, then ${facts["esm-cdn"].facts.warmMs} ms`,
  "user-code": `a React + TSX app served from snapshot ${facts["user-code"].facts.snapshot}`,
  bundler: `a Hono + Durable Object Worker bundled in ${facts.bundler.facts.buildMs} ms`,
  astro: `an Astro app built from its sources in ${(Number(facts.astro.facts.buildMs) / 1000).toFixed(1)} s`,
  notebooks: `two notebooks compiled in ${facts.notebooks.facts.buildMs} ms`,
};
---

<Docs
  title="Builders"
  description="The builders of the CellB platform, with working examples: npm packages as ESM, a project's code as ESM, a bundler for Workers with Durable Objects, an Astro builder and notebooks."
>
  <h1>Builders: from sources to running code</h1>
  <p>
    The platform turns code as people (and agents) write it into code a browser or a Worker runs.
    It has five builders. Each one is a Durable Object app, so it runs wherever CellB projects run:
    on Wrangler (workerd, as on Cloudflare), on celld, on CellB in Node, and on CellB in a browser
    tab. What a builder makes runs on the same hosts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each page of this section explains what the builder is for and how it works. It shows the
    example's input files, the code that uses the builder, and the results: the platform's real
    answers and screenshots of what the example produced.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {builderPages.map((p) => (
      <a href={p.href} class="group flex flex-col rounded-xl border no-underline bg-card p-4 shadow-xs transition-colors hover:border-primary/40 hover:bg-accent/40">
        <span class="font-medium text-foreground group-hover:text-primary">{p.label}</span>
        <span class="mt-1 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">{p.summary}</span>
        <span class="mt-3 font-mono text-[11px] leading-5 text-muted-foreground">
          <span class="text-foreground/70">in:</span> {p.input}<br />
          <span class="text-foreground/70">out:</span> {p.output}
        </span>
        <span class="mt-3 border-t pt-2 text-xs text-muted-foreground">Recorded: {stat[p.slug]}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>How they fit together</h2>
  <p>
    All five read npm packages through one package store, a caching proxy of the npm registry
    with one Durable Object per package. A package is downloaded once per deployment, whichever
    builder asks for it first.
  </p>
  <Flow
    steps={[
      { label: "registry.npmjs.org", detail: "packuments and tarballs, fetched once", kind: "external" },
      { label: "PackageRegistry", detail: "the package store: packuments for 10 minutes, tarballs forever" },
      { label: "ModuleHost · Project", detail: "module by module: the ESM CDN and user code as ESM" },
      { label: "BuildJob · NotebookSite", detail: "whole projects: the bundler, the Astro builder, notebooks" },
    ]}
    caption="The objects of the platform. Each keeps its files in its own SQLite (ctx.storage.sql), as blocks of at most 256 KiB."
  />
  <p>They take one of two approaches:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Module by module</th><th>One bundle</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Builders</td><td>ESM CDN, user code as ESM; the notebooks' imports</td><td>Bundler, Astro builder</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Built on</td><td><code>@statewalker/webrun-modules</code> (sucrase, Lightning CSS)</td><td><code>@statewalker/webrun-bundler</code> on esbuild-wasm</td></tr>
      <tr><td>When the work happens</td><td>When a module is first requested; then it is cached</td><td>Once per build; the build id is the hash of the inputs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What you get</td><td>URLs a browser imports; pinned and immutable</td><td>A folder: <code>wrangler.json</code>, <code>worker.js</code>, assets, as a ZIP or a Worker Loader payload</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Pages, editors, playgrounds: change one file, recompile one module</td><td>Workers and Durable Objects to deploy, store or load at run time</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Run the platform yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The examples talk to the platform at <code>http://localhost:8787</code>, the port{" "}
    <code>wrangler dev</code> uses. Any of these starts it there, from the repository root:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`pnpm install
cd packages/platform
pnpm run build                                    # dist/platform/workerd and dist/platform/cellb

npx wrangler dev                                  # on workerd, from the sources
npx tsx scripts/serve-cellb.mjs dist/platform/cellb --port 8787   # on CellB in Node
pnpm run build:browser && python3 -m http.server -d dist/browser 8787   # in a tab: open /__cellb/`}
  />
  <p>
    Then <code>curl localhost:8787/</code> lists its routes. The HTTP API of every builder is on{" "}
    <a href="/docs/tools">Tools: ESM CDN, bundler, builders</a>, and each package's README has its
    functions:
    {builderPages.map((p, i) => (
      <>
        {i > 0 && ", "}
        <a href={docHref(p.readme)}>{p.label}</a>
      </>
    ))}.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the results come from</h2>
  <p>
    The results on these pages are not written by hand.{" "}
    <a href={repoFile("site/scripts/record-builders.ts")}>scripts/record-builders.ts</a> starts
    the platform on CellB in Node from empty storage. It then sends each page's requests and opens
    the pages the examples produced in Chromium, checking them there (the chart is drawn, the
    island hydrates, the input re-runs the cells). It saves the answers to{" "}
    <code>site/src/builders/recorded/</code> and the screenshots to <code>site/public/builders/</code>.
    The pages read those files when the site is built. To record them again:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-site record:builders              # every example
pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-site record:builders bundler      # one`}
  />
  <p>
    The same builders are checked on every host by the platform's end-to-end scripts, as{" "}
    <a href={docHref("packages/platform/README.md")}>the platform's README</a> describes, and the
    larger apps they build (EmDash among them) are in{" "}
    <a href={docHref("examples/platform-demos/README.md")}>Apps built by the tools</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
